city": "Numerous flat, well-used sites strung along several miles of valley floor and its side creeks; there is no quota and no realistic crowding, so a party can nearly always find its own ground"}, {"elev": 6200, "name": "Sherpa south basin camp", "type": "camp", "notes": "Sitting a couple of thousand feet above the Ingalls Creek trail in the cirque south of Sherpa, this is where a party wanting a reasonable summit day on the East or West Ridge sleeps. Getting here means leaving the valley trail and grinding up open slopes and gullies with no path to speak of, so the climb from the creek is the crux of the day rather than the ridge. The payoff is that the East Ridge start is an easy morning from here instead of a pre-dawn thrash. It is also the natural base for the Northeast Couloir on Sherpa Balanced Rock, which is reached by continuing up the basin to the notch east of the balanced block. Ground is a mix of scree and low heather and level spots take some hunting; the basin is open and catches wind.", "water": "Snowmelt streams draining the basin are reliable into midsummer and thin out by September; late-season parties should fill on the way up from Ingalls Creek rather than assume", "permit": "Cle Elum Ranger District, Alpine Lakes Wilderness, outside the Enchantment Permit Area. Free self-issued trailhead permit for day and overnight use, no lottery, no quota. Northwest Forest Pass at Esmeralda. No campfires at this elevation, and pitch on gravel or bare rock rather than on the meadow.", "capacity": "A small number of established spots on gravel and heather benches; two parties is comfortable, three is crowded"}, {"elev": 7400, "name": "Table Rock bivy, upper Sherpa basin", "type": "bivy", "notes": "A large flat block high in the basin below Sherpa''s south side, used by parties who want the East Ridge to start within minutes of waking rather than an hour and a half above camp. From here the route climbs a broad gully trending north-northeast to gain the crest east of the balanced rock, then follows blocks and sandy benches west past the block to the summit. It is exposed, windy and cold compared with the basin camp a thousand feet lower, and whether the extra height is worth carrying water up for is a real judgement call rather than an obvious yes. Anyone attempting Sherpa Balanced Rock''s Northeast Couloir gets the most from this position, since the couloir tops out close by. Leave no trace of a platform; the rock is bare and the ground around it is fragile.", "water": "None dependable. Snow patches persist into midsummer and can be melted, but treat it as a dry bivy and carry water up from the basin below.", "permit": "Cle Elum Ranger District, free self-issued Alpine Lakes Wilderness permit, no Enchantment lottery involved. Northwest Forest Pass at Esmeralda if you came over Longs Pass. No fires, and nothing here to build one with.", "capacity": "Two to four people on flat rock; this is a perch rather than a campsite, and there is nothing else level nearby"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_sherpa_peak_west_ridge';

-- wa_sherpa_peak_north_ridge: bivy pruned 8->4, identical corridor-contamination pattern as the East Ridge and Northeast Couloir routes above.
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"name": "Ingalls Creek valley camps below the Sherpa and Argonaut south sides", "type": "camp", "notes": "This is the base for the south side of both Argonaut and Sherpa, and it is what makes those peaks feasible as overnights rather than brutal single pushes. Two ways in. From Esmeralda you climb to Longs Pass at about 6,200 feet and drop roughly 1,400 feet to the valley, then walk east along the trail until you are under your peak; Sherpa''s south basin is about a mile down, Argonaut''s exit gully perhaps three. From the low trailhead off US 97 you walk in flat but far, upwards of ten miles to reach the same ground, which trades the pass for distance and suits a party with time. A third link comes over Fourth Creek from the Beverly Creek trailhead in the Teanaway and requires fording Ingalls Creek, which is genuinely dangerous in June. The valley is low and sheltered, so it is buggy through midsummer and warm at night, and the brush between the trail and the open slopes above is the real work of the approach. Sites are dispersed rather than built, so pick an existing one instead of clearing new ground. The elevation of the trail changes across the several miles covered here, so no single figure is given.", "water": "Ingalls Creek runs strongly all season and side streams come in from the north wall; the only dry stretch is the climb over Longs Pass, which carries no water at all in either direction", "permit": "Alpine Lakes Wilderness under the Cle Elum Ranger District, entirely OUTSIDE the Enchantment Permit Area. A free self-issued wilderness permit from the trailhead box covers day and overnight use, there is no lottery, no quota and no reservation. A Northwest Forest Pass or day fee is needed to park at Esmeralda; the low Ingalls Creek trailhead off US 97 has its own parking requirement. Campfires are restricted seasonally and are a poor idea in any case; the valley is dry and forested. Camp at least 200 feet from the creek.", "capacity": "Numerous flat, well-used sites strung along several miles of valley floor and its side creeks; there is no quota and no realistic crowding, so a party can nearly always find its own ground"}, {"elev": 6200, "name": "Sherpa south basin camp", "type": "camp", "notes": "Sitting a couple of thousand feet above the Ingalls Creek trail in the cirque south of Sherpa, this is where a party wanting a reasonable summit day on the East or West Ridge sleeps. Getting here means leaving the valley trail and grinding up open slopes and gullies with no path to speak of, so the climb from the creek is the crux of the day rather than the ridge. The payoff is that the East Ridge start is an easy morning from here instead of a pre-dawn thrash. It is also the natural base for the Northeast Couloir on Sherpa Balanced Rock, which is reached by continuing up the basin to the notch east of the balanced block. Ground is a mix of scree and low heather and level spots take some hunting; the basin is open and catches wind.", "water": "Snowmelt streams draining the basin are reliable into midsummer and thin out by September; late-season parties should fill on the way up from Ingalls Creek rather than assume", "permit": "Cle Elum Ranger District, Alpine Lakes Wilderness, outside the Enchantment Permit Area. Free self-issued trailhead permit for day and overnight use, no lottery, no quota. Northwest Forest Pass at Esmeralda. No campfires at this elevation, and pitch on gravel or bare rock rather than on the meadow.", "capacity": "A small number of established spots on gravel and heather benches; two parties is comfortable, three is crowded"}, {"elev": 7400, "name": "Table Rock bivy, upper Sherpa basin", "type": "bivy", "notes": "A large flat block high in the basin below Sherpa''s south side, used by parties who want the East Ridge to start within minutes of waking rather than an hour and a half above camp. From here the route climbs a broad gully trending north-northeast to gain the crest east of the balanced rock, then follows blocks and sandy benches west past the block to the summit. It is exposed, windy and cold compared with the basin camp a thousand feet lower, and whether the extra height is worth carrying water up for is a real judgement call rather than an obvious yes. Anyone attempting Sherpa Balanced Rock''s Northeast Couloir gets the most from this position, since the couloir tops out close by. Leave no trace of a platform; the rock is bare and the ground around it is fragile.", "water": "None dependable. Snow patches persist into midsummer and can be melted, but treat it as a dry bivy and carry water up from the basin below.", "permit": "Cle Elum Ranger District, free self-issued Alpine Lakes Wilderness permit, no Enchantment lottery involved. Northwest Forest Pass at Esmeralda if you came over Longs Pass. No fires, and nothing here to build one with.", "capacity": "Two to four people on flat rock; this is a perch rather than a campsite, and there is nothing else level nearby"}, {"elev": 5400, "name": "Upper Mountaineer Creek bench", "type": "camp", "notes": "The north-side base for everything on this side of the range: the Sherpa Glacier and Sherpa''s north routes, Argonaut''s north approaches, and the start of the northeast ridge on Cannon Mountain. Leave the Stuart Lake trail at the first switchback where it begins to climb, roughly half a mile past the Colchuck Lake junction, and follow the creek up on its right for three to four miles on a rough climbers path. The bench at this elevation is the obvious flat ground and everyone stops there. Road access is the seasonal constraint: Forest Road 7601 off Icicle Creek Road is gated through winter and typically does not open until well into spring, which shortens the north-side season considerably compared with the Teanaway. Snow lingers in this basin, which is exactly why the couloir routes above are early-season objectives.", "water": "Mountaineer Creek runs beside the camp all season and is easy to draw from where the channel is open; in early season find a spot where the creek is running clear of snow", "permit": "This is the ambiguous one and it matters. The Stuart Lake trailhead sits inside the Enchantment Permit Area, and the Stuart Zone requires an overnight permit from May 15 through October 31, drawn either through the February advance lottery on the federal reservation system or through the daily lottery run from Leavenworth. Where the zone boundary falls in the upper Mountaineer Creek basin above the lakes is not clearly drawn on any public map, and reasonable people read it differently. Do not guess. Call the Wenatchee River Ranger District before the trip, and if you cannot get an answer, carry a Stuart Zone permit. Day use anywhere in the permit area needs only the free self-issued slip at the trailhead. Dogs are prohibited throughout the permit area and campfires are banned.", "capacity": "Plenty of good spots spread along the bench near the creek; several parties can spread out without seeing each other"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_sherpa_peak_north_ridge';

-- wa_sherpa_peak_north_ridge: removed a stale data_quality.gaps entry claiming 'This entry duplicates wa_north_ridge_9; catalog likely has two records for the same physical line.' Checked directly against the live database: no route with id wa_north_ridge_9 exists anywhere in the catalog (only unrelated co_north_ridge_9 in Colorado and ca_north_ridge_9 in California), and area wa_sherpa_peak has exactly one North Ridge route (this one) with no duplicate. The claim is false as written and was left un-updated after whatever earlier cleanup it referred to.
UPDATE routes SET data_quality = '{"gaps": ["No public GPS track found for this route as of this research pass.", "Difficulty breakdown (physical/technical/exposure/commitment/routefinding) is a computed starting estimate derived from grade, pitch count, and route data on file -- not a researched or crowd-sourced rating. Users can blend in their own read via the UI."], "confidence": "MEDIUM", "lastVerified": "2026-07-02"}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_sherpa_peak_north_ridge';

-- wa_silver_star_glacier: access.passRequired corrected from 'None' to state the Northwest Forest Pass requirement -- this contradicted every other field on the same row: access.fees, access.parking_pass ('Northwest Forest Pass required at Washington Pass Overlook, Blue Lake, and Cutthroat trailheads'), the trailhead waypoint's own note ('Northwest Forest Pass needed to park'), the approach text, and pro_tips ('Carry a Northwest Forest Pass') all agree a pass is required to park at the SR-20 pullout.
UPDATE routes SET access = jsonb_set(access, '{passRequired}', '"Northwest Forest Pass (or America the Beautiful pass) required to park at the SR-20 pullout \u2014 $5/day or $30/annual"'::jsonb) WHERE id = 'wa_silver_star_glacier';

-- wa_silver_star_glacier: waypoints[1] ('Early Winters Creek crossing') elev corrected from 4450 to 4200 ft. This row's own approach text and approach_variants text (two independent passages on the same row) both describe dropping 'roughly 150-200 ft down' loose ground from the SR-20 pullout (elev 4400) to reach the creek crossing 0.1 mi later -- the stored 4450 has the creek 50 ft HIGHER than the trailhead, the opposite of what the row's own prose describes. 4200 ft (a ~200 ft drop) is consistent with the stated range.
UPDATE routes SET waypoints = jsonb_set(waypoints, '{1,elev}', '4200'::jsonb) WHERE id = 'wa_silver_star_glacier';

-- wa_silver_star_glacier: gain_ft corrected from 4000 to 4400 to match this row's own itinerary day-by-day sum (day1 gainFt 3500 + day2 gainFt 900 = 4400) and its own loss_ft (4400, already equal to the itinerary's day-by-day loss sum of 0+4400=4400) -- descent_text explicitly describes this as a round trip reversing the ascent route back to the same trailhead, so total gain must equal total loss.
UPDATE routes SET gain_ft = 4400 WHERE id = 'wa_silver_star_glacier';

-- [OUT-OF-BATCH cross-reference fix] wa_colfax_peak_cosley_houston (Colfax Peak): bivy pruned 6->1. Same corridor-wide bivy array as Sherman Peak/Hadley Peak (found while auditing this batch's Sherman Peak routes -- see wa_sherman_peak_baker_route above for the full contamination description). This route was checked in an earlier batch this pass; the cross-peak duplication was not visible without comparing against the sibling peaks' rows. Kept only the one entry that explicitly names Colfax's own north-side ice routes ('Colfax's three north-side ice lines -- the Cosley-Houston, the Polish Route and Kimchi Suicide Volcano -- all start off the Coleman').
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 7500, "name": "Coleman Glacier bivy under the Black Buttes north faces", "type": "bivy", "notes": "Colfax''s three north-side ice lines — the Cosley-Houston, the Polish Route and Kimchi Suicide Volcano — all start off the Coleman somewhere near 8,000 ft, and this is the staging ground for a party that does not want to cover the whole approach from the Hogsback on climb morning. The important thing about it is where you do not put the tent: ice and rock come off the Colfax seracs onto this part of the glacier with some regularity, so the usable camping is out on the flats well clear of the fall line, never tucked in against the toe of the face because it looks sheltered. Many parties skip this camp entirely and climb these routes in a long day from the trailhead or from the Hogsback, which is a reasonable answer to the same hazard. These are cold-season routes, roughly late October into May, and the ice changes character completely across that window. Access is the current constraint rather than the camp: Glacier Creek Road washed out in the December 2025 floods and has been closed to vehicles well below the trailhead, open to walkers and bikes at the contractors'' discretion with repairs anticipated through 2026 — confirm the road before planning to carry a heavy load.", "water": "None to rely on. Melt snow. Surface runoff sometimes appears on the rock of the buttes in a hot spell and should not be planned around.", "permit": "Mt Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest, Mount Baker Wilderness. No climbing permit and no overnight permit; camping is dispersed. Northwest Forest Pass or Interagency pass to park at Heliotrope Ridge. Party limit 12, no campfires, no toilets on the route, waste packed out in blue bags available free from the ranger offices. Registering a climb is voluntary and worth doing.", "capacity": "Dug platforms for a party or two on the flats out from the foot of the faces; no established site and nothing to shelter behind"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_colfax_peak_cosley_houston';

-- [OUT-OF-BATCH cross-reference fix] wa_colfax_peak_polish_route (Colfax Peak): bivy pruned 6->1. Same corridor-wide bivy array as Sherman Peak/Hadley Peak (found while auditing this batch's Sherman Peak routes -- see wa_sherman_peak_baker_route above for the full contamination description). This route was checked in an earlier batch this pass; the cross-peak duplication was not visible without comparing against the sibling peaks' rows. Kept only the one entry that explicitly names Colfax's own north-side ice routes ('Colfax's three north-side ice lines -- the Cosley-Houston, the Polish Route and Kimchi Suicide Volcano -- all start off the Coleman').
