-- [OUT-OF-BATCH cross-reference fix] wa_colfax_peak_kimchi_suicide_volcano (Colfax Peak): bivy pruned 6->1. Same corridor-wide bivy array as Sherman Peak/Hadley Peak (found while auditing this batch's Sherman Peak routes -- see wa_sherman_peak_baker_route above for the full contamination description). This route was checked in an earlier batch this pass; the cross-peak duplication was not visible without comparing against the sibling peaks' rows. Kept only the one entry that explicitly names Colfax's own north-side ice routes ('Colfax's three north-side ice lines -- the Cosley-Houston, the Polish Route and Kimchi Suicide Volcano -- all start off the Coleman').
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 7500, "name": "Coleman Glacier bivy under the Black Buttes north faces", "type": "bivy", "notes": "Colfax''s three north-side ice lines — the Cosley-Houston, the Polish Route and Kimchi Suicide Volcano — all start off the Coleman somewhere near 8,000 ft, and this is the staging ground for a party that does not want to cover the whole approach from the Hogsback on climb morning. The important thing about it is where you do not put the tent: ice and rock come off the Colfax seracs onto this part of the glacier with some regularity, so the usable camping is out on the flats well clear of the fall line, never tucked in against the toe of the face because it looks sheltered. Many parties skip this camp entirely and climb these routes in a long day from the trailhead or from the Hogsback, which is a reasonable answer to the same hazard. These are cold-season routes, roughly late October into May, and the ice changes character completely across that window. Access is the current constraint rather than the camp: Glacier Creek Road washed out in the December 2025 floods and has been closed to vehicles well below the trailhead, open to walkers and bikes at the contractors'' discretion with repairs anticipated through 2026 — confirm the road before planning to carry a heavy load.", "water": "None to rely on. Melt snow. Surface runoff sometimes appears on the rock of the buttes in a hot spell and should not be planned around.", "permit": "Mt Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest, Mount Baker Wilderness. No climbing permit and no overnight permit; camping is dispersed. Northwest Forest Pass or Interagency pass to park at Heliotrope Ridge. Party limit 12, no campfires, no toilets on the route, waste packed out in blue bags available free from the ranger offices. Registering a climb is voluntary and worth doing.", "capacity": "Dug platforms for a party or two on the flats out from the foot of the faces; no established site and nothing to shelter behind"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_colfax_peak_kimchi_suicide_volcano';

-- [OUT-OF-BATCH cross-reference fix] wa_hadley_peak_cougar_divide (Hadley Peak): bivy pruned 6->3. Same corridor-wide bivy array as Sherman Peak/Colfax Peak (found while auditing this batch's Sherman Peak routes). Checked in an earlier batch this pass; not visible without comparing against the sibling peaks' rows. Kept the three entries that explicitly name Hadley Peak's own two approaches (Cougar Divide, Skyline Divide) and the basin connecting them.
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 5100, "name": "Cougar Divide meadow camps", "type": "camp", "notes": "This is the north-side approach to Hadley Peak and it has nothing to do with the Black Buttes or the Coleman — a party here is on the far side of the mountain from the Heliotrope Ridge climbers. Cougar Divide is an unofficial, unmaintained path along the ridge crest, reached from Wells Creek Road, whose first several miles are badly potholed to the Nooksack bridge and then improve. From the meadows at the head of the divide the ground opens onto Chowder Ridge, and the connection east along the ridge leads to Hadley. The gate opening in July combined with mosquitoes that are genuinely bad here until midsummer pushes the sensible window to mid-August through early October, which is also when the talus is clear and the route is straightforward walking with the odd second-class step.", "water": "Better than anywhere else on this side. A creek fed by a lingering snowfield runs at the upper camp through most of the season, and there are ponds along the divide that are worth treating and not drinking untreated. Water gets scarce once the snowfield goes.", "permit": "Mt Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest, Mount Baker Wilderness. No overnight permit and no climbing permit. The access road is gated seasonally, closed from the start of November until the second week of July for wildlife, which is the single biggest planning constraint on this side of the mountain. Party size 12, no campfires, pack out all waste.", "capacity": "A handful of small sites in the meadows near the head of the divide; pick rocky ground rather than heather, which recovers slowly here"}, {"elev": 6000, "name": "Skyline Divide crest camps", "type": "camp", "notes": "The other approach to Hadley Peak, from the west along the divide and over Chowder Ridge. The road in has been the problem for years rather than the trail: flood damage at around mile three has left the trailhead inaccessible to vehicles for extended periods with no firm repair date, and the trail has been listed as closed on that account, so this is the first thing to confirm and not an afterthought. When it goes, the crest is easy walking with big views and the last three quarters of a mile up to Chowder Ridge is talus with one steeper step before you top out west of the Hadley Glacier. Camping on the open crest means taking whatever weather arrives off the mountain, and the exposure is the reason parties often drop into the basin instead.", "water": "None at the trailhead and none along the trail after early summer, when the snow patches below the crest are gone. Carry everything or plan the trip around a lingering snowfield you have confirmed is still there.", "permit": "Mt Baker-Snoqualmie National Forest, Mount Baker Wilderness. A recreation pass is required at the trailhead. No campfires anywhere, party size capped at 12, all waste packed out. There is no permit or quota for camping, but the meadows are heavily used and heavily damaged, so camp on bare ground.", "capacity": "Several exposed sites along the open crest of the divide once the trail tops out; popular and visible, with no screening from wind or weather"}, {"elev": 5300, "name": "Deadhorse Creek basin, below Chowder Ridge", "type": "camp", "notes": "The basin sits between Skyline Divide and Cougar Divide, just short of the climb onto Chowder Ridge, and it solves the problem both crest camps have: it has water and it has shelter. A party approaching Hadley from either road can drop into it, sleep out of the wind, and take the ridge in the morning with full bottles. Summit day from here is short by any standard on this mountain. It is subalpine meadow and creek terrain, so camp on durable ground well back from the water. Season is the same as everything else on the north side, mid-August into early October, held back at the front end by mosquitoes and by the road gate on the Cougar Divide side and at the back end by the first real snow.", "water": "The most reliable water on the Hadley approach — the creek runs from snowmelt above the basin and holds up later than anything on the ridge crests. Treat it. This is the reason to camp here rather than higher.", "permit": "Mount Baker Wilderness, no overnight permit required. Recreation pass at whichever trailhead you start from. Party size 12, campfires prohibited, human waste and everything else packed out — there are no toilets on either divide.", "capacity": "Scattered informal sites in the basin between the two divides; sheltered, low use, no facilities of any kind"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_hadley_peak_cougar_divide';

-- [OUT-OF-BATCH cross-reference fix] wa_hadley_peak_skyline_divide (Hadley Peak): bivy pruned 6->3. Same corridor-wide bivy array as Sherman Peak/Colfax Peak (found while auditing this batch's Sherman Peak routes). Checked in an earlier batch this pass; not visible without comparing against the sibling peaks' rows. Kept the three entries that explicitly name Hadley Peak's own two approaches (Cougar Divide, Skyline Divide) and the basin connecting them.
